~ questions2
Shopkeeper: ... 
- Were you waiting for me last night? Didn't even get to properly knock before you opened the door. [if Variables.get_char_var("NPC_4", "wait") == false]
	Shopkeeper: [#sprite=worried]Oh, well, uh, kind of. I'm sorry if that was weird. [do Variables.mod_char_var("NPC_4", "questions", 1)][do Variables.set_char_var("NPC_4", "wait", true)]
	Shopkeeper: It's just that I've known for a while someone bearing the Gift would come, and yesterday I felt this urge to look out the window right before you came into view.
	Shopkeeper: And there you were. As soon as I saw you, I somehow knew it was you. Can't explain it more than that, though I wish I could.
	Shopkeeper: Plus, I didn't want your knocking to wake Sarah up, truth be told. She's a light sleeper. 
	- To be honest, I, myself, kind of knew this was your house even before Joan pointed it to me.
		Shopkeeper: There you have it. => questions2
	- Err, not sure how I feel about that. 
		Shopkeeper: I know I'm being odd again. But it's the truth. => questions2
- How do you know so much about me already? [if Variables.get_char_var("NPC_4", "know") == false]
	Shopkeeper: Your arrival was heralded by the Elders. [do Variables.mod_char_var("NPC_4", "questions", 1)][do Variables.set_char_var("NPC_4", "know", true)]
	Shopkeeper: They told me someone bearing the Gift would soon come to help me around with the shop. Goddess knows I could use it.
	Shopkeeper: To be completely honest, though, you never know with them. Their predictions have been wrong just as often as they've been right.
	Shopkeeper: [#sprite=worried]...Make sure not to tell anyone I said that, please. 
	Shopkeeper: I've got my own connection with the Goddess, though. She told me what I needed to know Herself.
	- She talked to me, too. In my sleep, in what felt like a dream. Told me to come here. 
		Shopkeeper: I'm well aware. She rarely does that, you know. 
		- Any idea why She chose me?
			Shopkeeper: None. She's not exactly straightforward, our Goddess. Few Gods ever are.
			Shopkeeper: All I know is you're important to her, and she wants me to train you. And for you to help me.
			Shopkeeper: And that's all I need to know for now. => questions2
		- Interesting. => questions2
	- I see. => questions2
- Is this village really unaffected by the Cullings? [if Variables.get_char_var("NPC_4", "unaffected") == false]
	Shopkeeper: Sure is. Not a single death, ever since they started occurring all those years ago. [do Variables.mod_char_var("NPC_4", "questions", 1)][do Variables.set_char_var("NPC_4", "unaffected", true)]
	Shopkeeper: The sky does turn red in the morning, heralding a Culling day, and we've had people die just outside our walls. 
	Shopkeeper: Gruesome, horrific way to go. Glad I've only witnessed it this one time.
	Shopkeeper: But inside, nothing. Not once. The Goddess cares for and protects us.
	- How is this possible?
		Shopkeeper: You'll have to see it to believe it. Perhaps, if you're a doubter now, those doubts will vanish then. 
		Shopkeeper: The Cullings are our Goddess's work. 
		Shopkeeper: It is punishment for humanity's sins over the years. And a way to cleanse the Earth and start anew.
		Shopkeeper: Sounds brutal, I know. But we, as a species, have been ruthless ourselves to this planet, our own home, driving it to a point where it was nearly uninhabitable anymore. 
		Shopkeeper: Something had to be done. 
		- I've seen countless people die horrible deaths. I can tell you, it is not worth it. 
			Shopkeeper: Who are we to say, at the end of the day? It's how things are now and we have to adjust to it, either way. => questions2
		- We had it coming. Hopefully some good comes out of it. 
			Shopkeeper: Time will tell. Either way, it's how things are now and we have to adjust to it. => questions2
	- Could it be that She's the one causing them, then?
		Shopkeeper: She sure is. It's a well known fact around here. 
		Shopkeeper: It is punishment for humanity's sins over the years. And a way to cleanse the Earth and start anew.
		Shopkeeper: [#sprite=worried]Sounds brutal, I know. But we ourselves, as a species, have been ruthless to this planet, our own home, driving it to a point where it was nearly uninhabitable anymore. 
		Shopkeeper: Something had to be done. 
		- I've seen countless people die horrible deaths. I can tell you, it is not worth it. 
			Shopkeeper:[#sprite=worried] Who are we to say, at the end of the day? It's how things are now and we have to adjust to it, either way. => questions2
		- We had it coming. Hopefully some good comes out of it. 
			Shopkeeper: [#sprite=worried]Time will tell. Either way, it's how things are now and we have to adjust to it. => questions2
- So, what's the deal with our so called Gift? [if Variables.get_char_var("NPC_4", "gift") == false]
	Shopkeeper: I'll explain it once we start working. It will be easier for you to understand. [do Variables.mod_char_var("NPC_4", "questions", 1)][do Variables.set_char_var("NPC_4", "gift", true)]
	Shopkeeper: The gist of it is, we get visions from the Goddess from time to time, and we can see more than other people can. 
	Shopkeeper: You'll see. => questions2
- Who are the "Elders"? [if Variables.get_char_var("NPC_4", "elders") == false]
	Shopkeeper: They're the founders and leaders of our wonderful village and church. [do Variables.mod_char_var("NPC_4", "questions", 1)][do Variables.set_char_var("NPC_4", "elders", true)]
	Shopkeeper: There's five of them, and they're the only ones who can communicate directly with the Goddess, besides me, of course. 
	Shopkeeper: They're very old, hence the name. They rarely come out of their home. They have underlings and messengers in their service, and that's how they usually communicate the Goddess's will and teachings to us.
	Shopkeeper: There's a chance you won't ever see them yourself, but it's safer to assume they're aware of your every move. 
	Shopkeeper: They don't take kindly to meddling newcomers. And they're not known for being forgiving to heretics and blasphemers. 
	Shopkeeper: Since you're not technically part of our church yet, the most severe form of punishment you could undergo is exile. 
	Shopkeeper: [#sprite=worried]But once you take the oath and become a fully fledged member, the punishment for sinning is either flogging, or death, depending on the severity of your actions. 
	Shopkeeper: Sounds worse than it is. All you have to do is not speak against or openly defy our Goddess. 
	- Sounds simple enough. => questions2
	- That's... fucked up. 
		Shopkeeper: [#sprite=worried]Salvation comes at a cost. => questions2
- Kind of feels like I'm joining a cult. [if Variables.get_char_var("NPC_4", "cult") == false]
	Shopkeeper: Eh, people here are just like everywhere else. [do Variables.mod_char_var("NPC_4", "questions", 1)][do Variables.set_char_var("NPC_4", "cult", true)]
	Shopkeeper: Some good apples, some bad ones. Perhaps a few rotten ones here and there. 
	Shopkeeper: There are some practices and beliefs which might seem... odd, especially to an outsider.
	Shopkeeper: Way I see it, at least they've got a purpose.
	Shopkeeper: Goes a long way, what with the state of the world right now and everything.
	- Guess I can respect that. 
		Shopkeeper: Just keep an open mind, yeah? => questions2
	- "They"? Can't relate?
		Shopkeeper: Sure can. Guess I've just been kind of by myself lately, focusing on Sarah and the shop. 
		Shopkeeper: [#sprite=worried]Easy to start feeling estranged, adopt an "us and them" mentality in such circumstances. 
		- I get it.
			Shopkeeper: [#sprite=laugh]Yeah. Hopefully you staying here will help prevent me from becoming a secluded hermit. Hah. => questions2
		- Would the Goddess approve?
			Shopkeeper: It was a slip of the tongue. The Goddess is forgiving. 
			Shopkeeper: [#sprite=worried]The Elders, not so much. I'd appreciate it if this and any further such mistakes on my part stayed between us.
			Shopkeeper: You scratch my back, I'll scratch yours, eh?
			- Sure thing. 
				Shopkeeper: Appreciate it.[do Variables.set_char_var("NPC_4", "relationship", 4)] => questions2
			- We'll see. 
				Shopkeeper: [#sprite=worried]Understandable. Guess I'll have to prove my worth first.[do Variables.set_char_var("NPC_4", "relationship", 2)] => questions2
	- Still feels weird. 
		Shopkeeper: I get it. I do. Just give it a bit of time. Besides, you've got me to help you settle in.[do Variables.mod_var("religious_fervour", -1)] => questions2
- What was your daughter talking about last night, with the dreams and everything? [if Variables.get_char_var("NPC_4", "dream") == false]
	Shopkeeper: [#sprite=worried]That's... complicated. [do Variables.mod_char_var("NPC_4", "questions", 1)][do Variables.set_char_var("NPC_4", "dream", true)]
	Shopkeeper: [#sprite=worried]She used to have nightmares every night a while back. It was a hard time for her. For us. 
	Shopkeeper: [#sprite=worried]Then they just... stopped.
	Shopkeeper: [#sprite=worried]Looks like they're a thing again. 
	Shopkeeper: [#sprite=worried]Her abundant imagination should be a source of joy, especially at her age. 
	Shopkeeper: [#sprite=worried]It's been anything but. 
	- I'm sorry to hear that. 
		Shopkeeper: [#sprite=worried]Yeah. Appreciate it. => questions2
	- What does she dream about?
		Shopkeeper: [#sprite=worried]It's... difficult to explain. 
		Shopkeeper: [#sprite=worried]She used to draw everything she remembered in the morning, then hang it up on the walls around the house. A few of them are still up, see?
		Shopkeeper: [#sprite=worried]Don't freak out if more weird drawings show up around the room in the future. 
		- Noted. => questions2
		- I'll... try. => questions2
- So, do you people sacrifice humans and stuff? [if Variables.get_char_var("NPC_4", "sacrifice") == false and Variables.get_char_var("NPC_4", "questions") >= 3] 
	Shopkeeper: [#sprite=worried]Uh, no, course not. [do Variables.set_char_var("NPC_4", "sacrifice", true)]
	- Okay. => questions2
	- You sure?
